 !!! Note
     The minimum last stake amount can be configured using `last_stake_amount_min_ratio` - which defaults to 0.5 (50%). This means that the minimum stake amount that's ever used is `stake_amount * 0.5`. This avoids very low stake amounts, that are close to the minimum tradable amount for the pair and can be refused by the exchange.
 
+#### Limiting exposure to a single underlying currency
+
+If you trade several pairs that share the same underlying currency (for example multiple pairs quoted in the same stablecoin, or several futures settled in the same currency), a large adverse move in that currency can hit several open trades at once. This concentration risk exists even though each trade looks fine in isolation.
+
+`max_single_currency_exposure` limits the combined stake amount of all simultaneously open trades that share the same underlying (base) currency, as a fraction of the [tradable capital](#tradable-balance). Pairs are grouped by their base currency - e.g. `BTC/USDT` and `BTC/USDT:USDT` are both grouped under `BTC`.
+
+For example, with `max_single_currency_exposure=0.3` and a tradable capital of 1000 USDT, no more than 300 USDT may be committed at any time across all open `BTC/...` trades combined. When opening (or increasing the size of) a trade would push the combined exposure above this limit, the stake amount for that trade is reduced to fit under the limit, or the trade is skipped entirely if no room is left. This is applied identically in live/dry-run and in backtesting.
+
+This setting is disabled (no cap) by default.
+
 #### Static stake amount
 
 The `stake_amount` configuration statically configures the amount of stake-currency your bot will use for each trade.
